/-!
# Eliminating the abelian branch of the `L₂(2)` case

Aschbacher–Smith, *The Classification of Quasithin Groups*, §14.6,
pp. 1020–1042 (combined 1 March 2004 manuscript).

The source extensions construct F.9.1 on the actual plane data and prove
14.6.1(4–6) and 14.6.2, together with the residual centralizer in 14.2.2(6).
The suitable-involution argument proves the indices, Q_H product and relative
normalizer in 14.6.3(1), the ambient normalizer in part (2), then the local Sylow
conclusions of 14.6.6(1,2,7) from the preceding ambient normalizers. The source
pair I, Mc satisfies 1.1.5; I ∩ Mc has characteristic two and O(I) = 1,
as in 14.6.7(1–3); part (4) places each component outside Mc and its
Sylow join back in the maximal outside family. The maximal-intersection
choices of 14.6.4–5 feed the actual residual and Sylow products in
14.6.6(3–5). The quotient-rank comparison of 14.6.8 and the order threshold
and normalizer control in 14.6.9 are also exported. The small residual-block
branch of 14.6.9 bounds the Sylow intersection by 2⁹ using original C.5
pushing-up and the actual maximal outside family.
When F*(I) differs from O₂(I), both original residual factorizations in
14.6.9 are proved. The same marked ordinary component and the rank-four
image of the original span in the parabolic quotient yield the contradiction
for every surviving model. When F*(I) equals O₂(I), the original C.5 orbit
and marked central involution exclude the natural block and leave the actual
paired-A₅ core and invariant family. The paired-A₅ centralizer contradiction
is now proved from that original configuration, completing both residual
factorizations in the characteristic-two branch.
The same chosen conjugator gives a residual-core intersection of order 16,
equal to its commutator subgroup. The original central-core quotient also
has characteristic two and the required Sylow omega-center containment.
The marked involution identifies each actual block intersection with Mc
as its binary A₄ extension, whose residual two-core has order 32 and
center order two. For a nontrivial residual centralizer D, its normalizer
has the same Sylow intersection and the original marked component data.
The Thompson comparison proves the point-core radicality, the containment
O₂(Mc) ≤ Rz and T ∩ I = T₀. The literal residuals of the block intersections
are identified with the two factors of O²(I ∩ Mc), and their two-cores
give its two-core product. The actual point-centralizer action supplies
the paired section of orders 64 and 4, with compatible coordinates and
irreducibility. The Thompson endpoint also excludes O₂(I) ≤ O₂(Mc).
After D vanishes, the literal point-core cover supplies the second
irreducible section. The quotient-center bound, paired commutators and
ordinary and modulo centralizer bounds then supply the endgame geometry.
The moved paired blocks centralize every ordinary component of N_G(D).
The odd-rank contradiction therefore makes that normalizer characteristic
two. The local short-block core containment and the Thompson endpoint
force D to vanish, without a separate factorization assumption on N_G(D).
The quotient omega-center containment places the actual irreducible section E₀
in the required normal preimage using the proved paired center bound.
The final plane-normalizer contradiction is proved from the literal
two-factor core decomposition, its swapping element, and the omega-center
order. For the residual cores of the actual block intersections with Mc,
their centers, disjointness, commutation and swapping element are derived
from the same source data. The two irreducible sections and centralizer
bounds prove their product equality with O₂(Mc) and close the contradiction.
The nontrivial-core branch of 14.6.6(6) constructs the generated choice
and feeds 14.6.10(1–2), proving the factorization and generation inputs.
The zero-core branch now constructs the two local characteristic-two
parabolics from the original data. The cubic F.1.12 size bound excludes
that branch and supplies the nontrivial core of the generated choice.
The local-Sylow universal-tree argument and the proved canonical labels
and radius restriction reduce this bound to the two earlier Gol80 marked
recognition inputs.
The local invariant-subgroup branches of 14.6.10(3) are also exported.
The full invariant-subgroup dichotomy and its normal-complement application
now use the completed14.6.9 source theorem: both products lift from a minimal
choice to every maximal-intersection outside subgroup with the same Sylow
intersection, with no remaining factorization callback.

For the same generated group, the selected C.2.7/F.6.18 component is now
proved to be its two-residual, supplemented by the source Sylow intersection.
The original first residual lies in that component, and the entire plane span
lies in its omega-center commutator module. In the genuine alternating-six
block branch, the full module has order 16 or 32. The source crossing argument
excludes the rank-five case and proves the second-parabolic span has rank three,
using the original preferred centralizer condition. The alternating-block
identification still requires reconstruction from the earlier star-image catalogue.
Independently, the actual A₆ star marking now bounds the generated source
span and its second-parabolic span by order 32. This uses the moved star
module and its genuine central quotient, without assuming whole-block
naturality. The required whole-core action remains a separate input to the
rank-five crossing argument.

The suitable involution normalizer conclusion 14.6.3(4) is proved, and its
local Sylow premise follows from the original local-normalizer condition.
The primitive odd-residual branch now supplies a transitive ratio-factor
family with at most two members; in the nonsingleton branch both odd-factor
intersections are cyclic. The exceptional singleton and the later suitable
involution selection, Goldschmidt-triple rank bound and final component
analysis are still required for Theorem 14.6.25.

In the cyclic-residual alternative, the original elementary closure and
index-five pair now supply a critical track on the literal generated
subgroup. Its seed and common Sylow map back to the original plane and T.
The positive critical distance and a minimizing vertex are proved without
additional geometric premises. The asymmetric marked enumeration and Tits
completion remain separate source obligations.
The canonical F.9 source setup further has odd critical distance at least
three and a normalized endpoint. In the index-five branch, one actual
conjugator gives the two module containments, the mutual commutator bound
and noncentral action on the marked central quotient, retaining the
elementary module of order 32 or 64.
-/
